{"name":"Silver Surfer","hex":"#7e7d88","fetched":"2026-10-16","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"silver surfer","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An elderly person who regularly uses the Internet."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/silver_surfer"}],"entries":[{"title":"Silver Surfer","blocks":[{"text":"The Silver Surfer is a superhero appearing in American comic books published by Marvel Comics. The character also appears in a number of movies, television, and video game adaptations. The character was created by Stan Lee and Jack Kirby and first appeared in the comic book Fantastic Four #48, published in 1966. The Silver Surfer is a humanoid alien with metallic skin who can travel through space with the aid of his surfboard-like craft. Originally a young astronomer named Norrin Radd on the planet Zenn-La, he saves his homeworld from the planet devourer, Galactus, by serving as his herald. Imbued in return with some portion of Galactus's Power Cosmic, he acquires vast power, a new body and a surfboard-like craft on which he can travel faster than light."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/3/34/Silver_Surfer.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Silver_Surfer","wikidata":"Q95153"},{"title":"Silver Surfer (comic book)","blocks":[{"text":"Silver Surfer or The Silver Surfer is the name of several series of comic books published by Marvel Comics featuring the Silver Surfer."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/9/9e/Silver_Surfer_01_cover.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Silver_Surfer_(comic_book)","wikidata":"Q7516437"},{"title":"Silver","blocks":[{"text":"Silver is a chemical element; it has symbol Ag and atomic number 47. A soft, white, lustrous transition metal, it exhibits the highest electrical conductivity, thermal conductivity and reflectivity of any metal. Silver is found in the Earth's crust in the pure, free elemental form, as an alloy with gold (electrum) and other metals, and in minerals such as acanthite, argentite and chlorargyrite. Most silver is recovered as a byproduct of lead–zinc, copper and gold mining and refining."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/55/Silver_crystal.jpg/330px-Silver_crystal.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Silver","wikidata":"Q1090"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/3/34/Silver_Surfer.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 1x","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/3/34/Silver_Surfer.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASilver_Surfer.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/9/9e/Silver_Surfer_01_cover.jpg/250px-Silver_Surfer_01_cover.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/9/9e/Silver_Surfer_01_cover.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/9/9e/Silver_Surfer_01_cover.jpg/250px-Silver_Surfer_01_cover.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASilver_Surfer_01_cover.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/55/Silver_crystal.jpg/500px-Silver_crystal.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/55/Silver_crystal.jpg/1280px-Silver_crystal.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/55/Silver_cr